
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- concepts/closure-properties
OK   example: (complement of {0^m1^n : m != n}) ∩ 0*1* = {0^n1^n}  (|w| <= 14)
OK   witness: {0^n1^n2^m} ∩ {0^m1^n2^n} = {0^n1^n2^n}  (|w| <= 11)
OK   {0^n1^n2^m} is generated by S -> AC, A -> 0A1 | ε, C -> 2C | ε  (|w| <= 9)
OK   {0^m1^n2^n} is generated by the symmetric grammar S -> ZB, Z -> 0Z | ε, B -> 1B2 | ε  (|w| <= 9)
OK   half witness L = {0^n1^n} 2* 0* {1^n2^n} is context-free (S -> AXYB, ...; |w| <= 9)
OK   half(L) ∩ 0+1+2+ = {0^n1^n2^n : n >= 1}  (|w| <= 10, so |ww| <= 20)
OK   h^{-1}(L): the DFA with delta'(q,a) = delta*(q,h(a)) accepts {w : h(w) ∈ L}  (200 random DFAs and h)
OK   h(L): substituting h(a) for a in a regular expression R gives h(L(R))  (5 expressions × 3 h, |u| <= 10)
OK   CF reversal: reversing every right side generates {w^R : w ∈ L(G)}  (4 grammars, |w| <= 8)
OK   CF ∩ regular (product with a DFA): {#0 = #1} ∩ 0*1* = {0^n1^n}  (|w| <= 10)
OK   CF ∩ regular: {0^m1^n : m != n} ∩ even length  (|w| <= 10)
OK   trap: L ∪ complement(L) = Σ* for L = {0^n1^n}, a regular union of two non-regular languages
OK   true/false: L \ R = L ∩ complement(R) is context-free, e.g. {#0 = #1} minus 'contains 11'  (|w| <= 10)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- concepts/fooling-sets
OK   #0 > #1: F = 0*, z = 1^min(i,j) distinguishes 0^i and 0^j  (all i != j <= 12)
OK   ... the string with min(i,j) zeros is out of L, the one with max(i,j) zeros is in L
OK   the naive suffix z = 1^i fails exactly when j < i (then neither xz nor yz is in L)  [78 failing pairs]
OK   {ε, 1, 11} is a fooling set for 'contains 11' with the suffixes 1, ε, ε
OK   ... so the 3-state DFA for 'contains 11' is minimal

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- concepts/myhill-nerode-theorem
OK   DFA remembering the last three symbols (padded with 0s) accepts 'third symbol from the end is 1'
OK   ... it has 8 states and is minimal: 2^3 = 8 classes
OK   x ≡ y iff x and y end in the same three padded symbols  (|x|, |y| <= 5, suffixes |z| <= 4)
OK   the 8 windows 000..111 are a fooling set: z = 0^(k-1), k = first position where u and v differ
OK   a 4-state NFA (loop at the start, guess the 1, read two more symbols) accepts the same language
OK   generally 'k-th symbol from the end is 1' needs 2^k DFA states (k = 1..5)
OK   trap: {0} over {0,1} has three classes [ε], [0], [1]; its minimal DFA has 3 states (with the dump state)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- concepts/dfa-minimization
OK   flag + last symbol: 5 reachable states (F,ε), (F,0), (F,1), (T,0), (T,1); the T states accept
OK   ... and it accepts exactly the strings containing 11
OK   step 2 marks the 6 accepting/non-accepting pairs
OK   pass 1 marks exactly {(F,ε),(F,1)} and {(F,0),(F,1)}
OK   ... both are sent by symbol 1 to the marked pair {(F,1),(T,1)}
OK   pass 2 marks nothing
OK   (F,ε),(F,0) and (T,0),(T,1) have identical successors
OK   merging leaves 3 states {(F,ε),(F,0)}, {(F,1)}, {(T,0),(T,1)}, equivalent to the 3-state DFA
OK   trap: §3.5's brute-force design has 2 × 7 = 14 possible states, 4 of them unreachable

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- concepts/language-transformations
OK   del0(0110) = 11
OK   {w : del0(w) ∈ L}: the 0-ignoring DFA is correct  (204 DFAs incl. ∅, {ε}, 1*, Σ*; |w| <= 10)
OK   {del0(w) : w ∈ L}: the guessing NFA is correct, exactly  (204 DFAs; |u| <= 8)
OK   trap: swapping the accepting states of an NFA for 'contains 1' does not give 0* (it accepts 1)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- concepts/context-free-grammars
OK   S -> AB, A -> 0A1 | ε, B -> 1B0 | ε generates {0^a 1^b 0^c : b = a + c}  (|w| <= 14)
OK   L(A) = {0^n1^n} and L(B) = {1^n0^n}  (|w| <= 14)
OK   0^a 1^(a+c) 0^c = 0^a 1^a • 1^c 0^c  (a, c <= 8)
OK   fooling set 0* for it: x = 0^i, y = 0^j, z = 1^i: xz ∈ L, yz ∉ L  (i != j <= 12)
OK   ... because every string in L has as many 1s as 0s  (|w| <= 14)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- concepts/parse-trees-and-ambiguity
OK   S -> 0S1 | 0S | ε generates {0^m1^n : m >= n}  (|w| <= 14)
OK   S -> 0S | T, T -> 0T1 | ε generates the same language  (|w| <= 14)
OK   001 has exactly two parse trees in the first grammar
OK   the two derivations of 001 on the page are leftmost derivations, and different
OK   0^m1^n has C(m, n) parse trees in the first grammar  (m + n <= 12, m >= n)
OK   every string of the language has exactly one parse tree in the second grammar  (|w| <= 12)
OK   1-1-1 grouped (1-1)-1 = -1 and 1-(1-1) = 1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- concepts/chomsky-normal-form
OK   palindrome grammar P -> 0P0 | 1P1 | 0 | 1 | ε  (|w| <= 12)
OK   after steps 0-1 (new start, split long rules): same language  (|w| <= 12)
OK   step 2: the nullable nonterminals are exactly P and S'
OK   after step 3 (ε-rules removed, S' -> ε added): same language  (|w| <= 12)
OK   final grammar is in CNF  [(True, None)]
OK   final grammar generates exactly the palindromes  (|w| <= 12)
OK   every parse tree of a palindrome of length n >= 1 has exactly 2n - 1 nonterminal nodes  (n <= 10)
OK   010: one parse tree, 5 nonterminal nodes (S', T0, X, P, T0)
OK   trap: a right side with k nullable symbols has 2^k variants (k = 3: ABC gives 8, one of them empty)
OK   trap: removing ε-rules turns A -> BC (B nullable, C not) into A -> BC | C, and A -> C is a unit rule

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- concepts/recursive-automata
OK   the one-module automaton s -(-> a -S-> b -)-> c -S-> t, s -ε-> t accepts the balanced strings (|w| <= 12)
OK   run on (): read (, call S (push b), ε to t, return to b, read ), call S (push t), ε, return; accept
OK   Lemma 5.6 gives [s] -> ([a] | [t], [a] -> [s][b], [b] -> )[c], [c] -> [s][t], [t] -> ε
OK   ... and that grammar generates exactly the balanced strings  (|w| <= 12)
OK   S -> (S)S | ε generates the balanced strings  (|w| <= 12)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- concepts/counting-arguments
OK   listing by length, then alphabetically: w_i is the binary numeral of i without its leading 1 (i <= 4095)
OK   w_1..w_6 = ε, 0, 1, 00, 01, 10 and 6 = 110 in binary
OK   diagonal against ∅, 0*, (0+1)*, {0^n1^n}: D contains ε and 00 but not 0 or 1

ALL OK
